  "text": "Solution 3 is the most likely to be correct, but I could not verify it, so my confidence is moderate.\n\n- **Solution 1 (answer 0)** only rules out the Mycielskian of $C_7$, the Grötzsch graph and the Chvátal graph, then asserts that systematic attempts \"lead to contradictions.\" That is not a proof, and a zero answer would need an exhaustive argument it does not give.\n- **Solution 2 (answer 1)** asserts that the Grötzsch graph has no induced $C_7$ without showing the case analysis. It then appeals to an unspecified classification to get exactly one graph. The argument is hand-waving, so I can't accept its count.\n- **Solution 3 (answer 2)** cites a specific source, Goedgebeur and Schaudt's exhaustive generation of $k$-vertex-critical $H$-free graphs, which I believe treats 4-critical $P_7$-free graphs. I recall that line of work giving a small finite list with a count of graphs containing an induced $C_7$, but I cannot confirm the exact number from memory. Of the three, its answer is the one that matches a known classification rather than an unsupported claim.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
